*******************************************************************************************                                                                                        **		Apple IIgs Printer Driver                                                        **                                                                                        **		File: communications.a -- PRINTER/MODEM port serial R/W to printer hardware      **                                                                                        **		Written by Ben Koning                                                            **		Copyright (C) 1991 Apple Computer, Inc.                                          **                                                                                        *******************************************************************************************							include		'equates.h'																												include		'm16.util'							include		'm16.misctool'CommunicationsProc			proc export**COMMUNICATIONS: APPLE CANON BJ PRINTER SPECIAL SERIAL READ/WRITE ROUTINES**These routines perform serial communications between the rest of the driver and the bubble jet*printer firmware.  The GS Firmware, Text Tool Set, and GS/OS are all bypassed; these routines*communicate directly to the SCC chip at 57.6K Baud with the special printer-style software*handshaking protocol.**The following entry points are implemented:**	CommGetWhichPort	Can be called anytime to determine which SCC channel we will use*	CommOpen			Must be called first; determines which SCC channel to use; initializes*	CommSetStatusProc	Sets up optional proc to be called with err code if pr err status detected*	CommGetAbort		Should be called to see if we want to abort and if data is still being sent*	CommWriteShort		Writes pstring pointed to by given 16-bit address*	CommWriteLong		Writes arbitrarily-sized data block*	CommReadByte		Attempts to read one byte*	CommClose			Must be called last to deallocate resources**Entry points that require pointers should be called with the low word in X and the high word*in Y.  Entry points that require input values should be called with the A register set.*Output, if any, is given in the A register and carry flag.**If an error occurs during the read or write calls, then subsequent read and write calls will*have no effect.  The routine CommGetAbort may be called at any time to determine the error*status; if it returns nonzero then data integrity has been lost.  The abort may also be set*as the result of certain printer status inquiries.**Whenever the write routines query the printer for its buffer capacity status (see below),*the printer status (paper out, etc) is checked.  If status is abnormal, then an optional*status proc (set by CommSetStatusProc) is called with an status value in A.  The status proc*should return 0/1 in A; if it returns 0 then the printer status is checked again, and*if a problems still exists then the proc is called again.  If the status proc returns 1 then*the communications error will be asserted, thus rendering the read/write calls inept and*communicating (via CommGetAbort) the abort request to the higher levels.**The possible values passed to the status proc are as follows:**	$01 = ready light is not on*	$02 = out of paper*	$8F = serious error (paper jam, carriage jam, logic test, ddr, buffer)**The SCC channel (A/B, alias Printer/Modem) is automatically elected when calling the CommOpen.*The CommGetWhichPort call is provided to infer which SCC channel to communicate through from the*name of the currently selected print-manager-printer-port driver.  This call may be made outside of*the scope of the Open/Read/Write/Close call sequence.**The printer has no hardware handshaking; its RS-232 handshake lines are always held high.*Thus, these routines have to know how large the printer buffer is and keep track of how many bytes*have been written, in order to avoid buffer overflow.  An initial assumption is made about the*amount of space available in the printer's receive buffer at "Open" time.  Every call to one of*the "Write" routines causes the internal notion of how much room is left to decrease.  When this*value becomes less than the length of a write request, the return-remaining-space immediate*printer command is isssued to get the latest availability, which is then used as the new value.*If there is not enough room in the printer to handle the current write request, the write routine*will wait until the situation changes (the printer is able to free up its receive buffer space*while it is printing).***COMMGETWHICHPORT: Returns if Control Panel "Printer"/"Modem" port is selected: A -> 0/1:*							export CommGetWhichPortCommGetWhichPort							pha									;get pointer to pstring with print							pha									;manager's port driver filename							ldx #$2913							;_PMGetPortName							jsl $E10000							pulllong ParamLongA							ldy #1								;access the first two characters							lda [ParamLongA],y					;of the name, one byte past len							;     7654321076543210				;clear bits 5 in both bytes, thus							and #%1101111111011111				;ensuring upper case							cmp #'M'+('O'*256)							beq	GeCpPoItsModem					;if first two chars of name are "MO"GeCpPoItsPrinterOrOther		lda #0								;then return "1"; in all other cases							rts									;return "0"GeCpPoItsModem				lda #1							rts**COMMOPEN: Call before any read/write calls: Error -> carry flag:*							export CommOpenCommOpen*Clear successful-open flag first:							stz	ConnectionIsOpen							*Reset status and timer flags first before proceeding:							stz ConnectionAbort					;clear abort flag							stz StatusProcPtr+0					;clear status proc ptr							stz StatusProcPtr+2*Ensure that the port we are using is not set to "your card" instead of built-in firmware:							jsr CommGetWhichPort				;set A reg to 0/1 if printer/modem							clc									;use A to select param #$21/#$22							adc #$21							pha									;pass result space							pha									;pass parameter number							_ReadBParam							pla									;get battery ram parameter byte							beq CoOpSlotCheckDone				;if it's set to 0 (internal f/w)							cmp #3								;or 3 (internal f/w reversed)							beq CoOpSlotCheckDone				;then it's ok, else error							brl CoOpExitErrorCoOpSlotCheckDone*Set up addresses to be used later to access SCC chip directly:							jsr CommGetWhichPort				;find which SCC channel desired							and #1								;we have A = 0)printer or 1)modem							eor #1								;so get X = 1)printer or 0)modem							tax									;and use it to generate SCC access							clc									;addresses: $C039=cmd + $C03B=data							adc #$C038							;for PRINTER port, or $C038=cmd +							sta SccCommandRegisterSPtr			;$C03A=data for MODEM port							txa							clc							adc #$C03A							sta SccDataRegisterSPtr							*Obtain information to call the serial firmware init routine:							txa									;X is still set 1/0 if pr/mo port							eor #1								;A now has 0/1 if pr/mo port							tay									;Y now has 0/1 if pr/mo for use below							xba									;A now has $0000/$0100 if pr/mo port							clc							adc #$C100							;A now has $C100/$C200 if pr/mo port							tax									;X now has $C100/$C200 if pr/mo port							lda >$E0000D,x						;get byte at $E0Cn0D, which is the							and #$00FF							;init-port-routine offset							stx ParamWordA						;put X ($C100/$C200) in dp var							clc									;so we can add init-port offset							adc ParamWordA						;A now has $CnXX addr of init routine							sta SerialFirmwareInitSPtr			;save this addr to use below+later							tya									;get 0/1 if pr/mo in A							clc							adc #$00C1							;A now has $C1/$C2 if pr/mo							sta SerialFirmwareInitXReg			;save this value to use below+later							tya									;get 0/1 if pr/mo in A							ina									;A now has 1/2 if pr/mo							asl a							asl a							asl a							asl a								;A now has $10/$20 if pr/mo							sta SerialFirmwareInitYReg			;save this value for use below+later*Call serial firmware initialization routine to set up the SCC:							lda #0							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;pass A input -- don't care							pushword SerialFirmwareInitXReg		;pass X input -- $00Cn							pushword SerialFirmwareInitYReg		;pass Y input -- $00n0							pushword SerialFirmwareInitSPtr		;pass $CnXX as firmware entry point							_FWEntry							;call the init routine							pla									;get Y output -- don't care							pla									;get X output -- don't care							pla									;get A output -- don't care							pla									;get P output -- don't care*Set the SCC's communication rate to 57.6K baud:							php									;disable interrupts for a moment							sei									;so nobody else can touch SCC							ldx SccCommandRegisterSPtr			;set appropriate SCC channel's							shortm								;write-registers 12 and 13 both							lda #12								;to 0, which sets the baud rate							sta >$E00000,x						;generator (16x mode) to 57.6KB							lda #0							sta >$E00000,x							lda #13							sta >$E00000,x							lda #0							sta >$E00000,x							longm							plp									;reenable interrupts*Initialize for keeping track of printer buffer space, using code from the write routine:							lda #CoOpGBSCmdEnd-CoOpGBSCmdBegin	;call the actual write routine in							ldx #CoOpGBSCmdBegin				;code below to send the return-							ldy #^CoWrGBSCmdBegin				;buff-space command							jsr CoWrDoWrite							jsr CommReadByte					;read the latest-availability-byte							bcs CoOpExitError					;if error exit and inform caller							xba									;returned byte bits should occupy							lsr a								;b6..b13 to regain proper value							lsr a							;	  5432109876543210							and #%0011111111000000							sec									;subtract out safety slop							sbc #CommDevBufferDrainSafety							bmi CoOpExitError					;error if can't take out slop							sta PrinterBufferSpaceLeft			;initialize our space-left value*Exit points:CoOpExitSuccess				clc									;no-error exit point: carry clear							ldx #1								;set the successful-open flag							stx ConnectionIsOpen							rts							CoOpExitError				sec									;if error: carry set							rts							*Immediate printer commands:CoOpGBSCmdBegin							dc.b $FF,$FF,$FF,$42CoOpGBSCmdEnd**COMMGETABORT: Gets abort status (0/1 is false/true), if any, in A:*							export CommGetAbortCommGetAbort							lda ConnectionAbort							rts**COMMSETSTATUSPROC: Sets optional proc to be called if pr status:  Pass lo/hi ptr -> X/Y:*							export CommSetStatusProcCommSetStatusProc							stx StatusProcPtr+0							sty StatusProcPtr+2							rts**COMMWRITESHORT: Sends the pstring pointed to by the given 16-bit pointer: Pass short pointer -> A:*							export CommWriteShortCommWriteShort							tax									;put short ptr (lo 16 bits) in X							lda |$0000,x						;get word with length byte in our							and #$00FF							;bank, isolate length byte							tay									;save length							inx									;point X at data beyond len byte							shortm								;go to 8 bit acc/mem mode							lda #0								;put a zero byte							pha									;on the stack							longm								;go back to 16 bit acc/mem mode							tya									;restore length							phb									;put current bank byte on stack							ply									;get $00nn word with bank in Y							bra CommWriteLong					;X/Y now has lo/hi ptr, A has length**COMMWRITELONG: Sends the given block of data bytes: Pass pointer lo/hi -> X/Y, length -> A:*							export CommWriteLongCommWriteLong*If zero-length write request, don't do anything:							cmp #0							bne CoWrDoneCheckNull							rtsCoWrDoneCheckNull*Setup temporary variables with given parameters:							sta ParamWordB						;pwB will have len of write request							stx ParamLongB+0					;plB will have ptr of write request							sty ParamLongB+2							*If an error has already occurred, don't do anything:							lda ConnectionAbort							beq CoWrDoneCheckError							rtsCoWrDoneCheckError*Check how much space we think is left in printer; if enough to do write request, do it:							lda PrinterBufferSpaceLeft							cmp ParamWordB							bcs CoWrThereIsEnoughSpace*Here if we have sent all the data we were autorized to send since last buffer-space check:CoWrInquireNewSpaceLeft		*Ask the printer how much space it actually has left right now in its buffer:							lda #CoWrGBSCmdEnd-CoWrGBSCmdBegin							ldx #CoWrGBSCmdBegin				;call the actual write routine to							ldy #^CoWrGBSCmdBegin				;send the return-buff-space command							jsr CoWrDoWrite														jsr CommReadByte					;read the latest availability-byte							xba									;returned byte's bits should occupy							lsr a								;b6..b13 to regain proper value							lsr a							;	  5432109876543210							and #%0011111111000000							sta PrinterBufferSpaceLeft*Check to see if there is any abnormal printer status at this time:							jsr CoWrCheckStatus					;do status check							lda ConnectionAbort					;if no chronic problem found go on							beq CoWrDoneCheckStatus				;else exit this routine with error							rtsCoWrDoneCheckStatus*Subtract from space-left given by printer as a safeguard against complete drain and leave in A:							lda PrinterBufferSpaceLeft							sec							sbc #CommDevBufferDrainSafety							bpl CoWrSetSmallerSpaceLeft							lda #0								;pin to 0, never make it negativeCoWrSetSmallerSpaceLeft		sta PrinterBufferSpaceLeft*Again check to see if there is enough room in space-left in A, and go do the write if it's ok:							cmp ParamWordB							bcs CoWrThereIsEnoughSpace*Else, wait a while to allow the printer to drain its buffer a bit and then try again:							lda #CommDevBufferDrainDelay							jsr CoWrWait							bra CoWrInquireNewSpaceLeft*Here if our counter indicates there is enough space in the printer to safely do the write:CoWrThereIsEnoughSpace*Subtract length of write request from our space-left counter:							lda PrinterBufferSpaceLeft							sec							sbc ParamWordB							sta PrinterBufferSpaceLeft*Set parameters and drop into subroutine which writes data and then exits:							lda ParamWordB						;pass the given len and ptr params							ldx ParamLongB+0					;into the routine below and drop							ldy ParamLongB+2					;through and exit*Subroutine to write data through the SCC to the printer: Pass lo/hi ptr -> X/Y, length -> A:CoWrDoWrite					sta ParamWordA						;pwA will have source length							stx ParamLongA+0					;plA will have source pointer							sty ParamLongA+2							shortm								;all A/memory operations are bytes							ldy #0								;init source data counter							CoWrDoWriteLoop				ldx SccCommandRegisterSPtr			;get command register addressCoWrDoWriteLoop1			php									;disable interrupts so control of							sei									;the SCC won't be taken away							lda #0								;accessing read-register-0							sta >$E00000,x							lda >$E00000,x						;if SCC channel is ready for a byte							;	  76543210						;break out if b2=Tx latch ready							and #%00000100							bne CoWrDoWriteLatchReady							plp									;else restore interrupts for a few							bra CoWrDoWriteLoop1				;cycles and try again							CoWrDoWriteLatchReady	 	lda [ParamLongA],y					;get source data byte and write							ldx SccDataRegisterSPtr				;it to SCC channel data latch							sta >$E00000,x							plp									;restore interrupts, allow SCC touch														iny									;increment source data counter							cpy ParamWordA						;go until all data is sent							bne CoWrDoWriteLoop					;length must be > 0														longm								;back to all-16-bit mode							rts									;exit when done*Subroutine to wait given number of ticks (1/60s): Pass number of ticks to wait -> A:CoWrWait					asl a								;convert ticks to 16ths ticks							asl a							asl a							asl a							tax														pha									;result space							pushword #0							;anchor value							phx									;number of 16ths of ticks to wait							ldx #$3A03							;_WaitUntil							jsl $E10000							pla									;ignore new anchor result														rts*Subroutine to check pr status and call proc with status code in A, proc returns OK/Cancel in A:CoWrCheckStatus				lda StatusProcPtr+0					;check if proc is installed							tax									;if none installed, just return							ora StatusProcPtr+2							bne CoWrCSGo1							rts							CoWrCSGo1					stx CoWrCSCallUserJSL+1+0			;setup JSL for callback							lda StatusProcPtr+2							shortm							sta CoWrCSCallUserJSL+1+2							longm							CoWrCSGo2					lda #CoWrSB1CmdEnd-CoWrSB1CmdBegin	;get status-byte-1							ldx #CoWrSB1CmdBegin							ldy #^CoWrSB1CmdBegin							jsr CoWrDoWrite							jsr CommReadByte														sta CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;save it for a moment														lda #CoWrSB2CmdEnd-CoWrSB2CmdBegin	;get status-byte-2							ldx #CoWrSB2CmdBegin							ldy #^CoWrSB2CmdBegin							jsr CoWrDoWrite							jsr CommReadByte														xba									;put SB 2 in hi byte							ora CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;put SB 1 in lo byte							sta CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;so now we have both bytes in word														lda CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;check for any of the following							;SB   2222222211111111				;being "1": SB1b1 (logic), SB2b4							;bit  7654321076543210				;(buff), SB2b6 (ddr), SB2b7 (cmd),							and #%1101100100000010				;SB2b0 (cjam), SB2b3 (pjam)							beq CoWrCSDoneFatal					;STATUS CODE: FATAL ERROR							lda #$8F							bra CoWrCSCallUserCoWrCSDoneFatal							lda CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;check for SB2b2 being "1"							;SB   2222222211111111				;STATUS CODE: OUT OF PAPER							;bit  7654321076543210							and #%0000010000000000							beq CoWrCSDoneNoPaper							lda #$02							bra CoWrCSCallUserCoWrCSDoneNoPaper							lda CoWrCSsb2andsb1					;check for SB1b0 being "0"							;SB   2222222211111111				;STATUS CODE: NOT IN READY							;bit  7654321076543210							and #%0000000000000001							bne CoWrCSDoneNotReady							lda #$01							bra CoWrCSCallUserCoWrCSDoneNotReady														rts									;here if no problem, return							CoWrCSCallUser				phb									;save env, call user, restore							phdCoWrCSCallUserJSL			jsl $FFFFFF							;NOTE: operand is stuffed							pld							plb														cmp #0								;see if user returned 0 in A							beq CoWrCSGo2						;if so, retry and maybe exit if ok							sta ConnectionAbort					;else, arrange to abort pretty soon							rts*Static variables:CoWrCSsb2andsb1				dc.w 0								;SB 2 in hi byte, SB 1 in lo byte*Immediate-response printer commands:CoWrGBSCmdBegin							dc.b $00,$FF,$FF,$FF,$42,$00		;return remaining buffer capacityCoWrGBSCmdEndCoWrSB1CmdBegin							dc.b $00,$FF,$FF,$FF,$31,$00		;return status byte 1CoWrSB1CmdEndCoWrSB2CmdBegin							dc.b $00,$FF,$FF,$FF,$32,$00		;return status byte 2CoWrSB2CmdEnd**COMMREADBYTE: Tries to input a byte: Returns A -> byte-value, carry set if timeout error:*							export CommReadByteCommReadByte							lda ConnectionAbort					;don't do anything if error status							beq CoRbDoneCheckError							sec							rtsCoRbDoneCheckError							lda #CommReadByteResponseDelay		;initial delay to wait for							jsr CoWrWait						;the printer to respond														ldy #CommReadByteTimeout			;init timeout count in Y							lda #0								;ensure upper byte of 16-bit A zero							shortm								;we want 8-bit memory fetches							ldx SccCommandRegisterSPtr			;SCC cmd reg address in X							CoRbWaitLoop				php									;disable interrupts so that no one							sei									;can change SCC unknown to us							dey									;decrement timeout countdown							beq CoRbExitError					;exit if we time out							lda #0								;accessing read-register-0							sta >$E00000,x							lda >$E00000,x							;	  76543210							and #%00000001						;b0 is receive-latch-full							bne CoRbByteReady					;break out if a byte is pending							plp									;else reenable interrupts for a							bra CoRbWaitLoop					;few cycles and try again							CoRbByteReady				ldx SccDataRegisterSPtr				;read from SCC data reg with byte							lda >$E00000,xCoRbExitNoError				plp									;reeneable interrupts, back to 16 bit							clc									;clear carry status							longm							rts							CoRbExitError				plp									;reeneable interrupts, back to 16 bit							sec									;set carry status to indicate error							longm								;also set communications error							ldx #1							stx ConnectionAbort							rts**COMMCLOSE: This routine must be called when all read/write activity is done:*							export CommCloseCommClose							lda ConnectionIsOpen				;don't do anything if never							beq CoClExit						;opened successfully														lda #0								;restore SCC by calling init routine							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;result space							pha									;pass A input -- don't care							pushword SerialFirmwareInitXReg		;pass X input -- $00Cn							pushword SerialFirmwareInitYReg		;pass Y input -- $00n0							pushword SerialFirmwareInitSPtr		;pass $CnXX as firmware entry point							_FWEntry							;call the init routine							pla									;get Y output -- don't care							pla									;get X output -- don't care							pla									;get A output -- don't care							pla									;get P output -- don't care														stz ConnectionIsOpen				;be sure to reset this now							CoClExit					rts**GLOBAL DATA: Private static data that is maintained across calls to these routines:*PrinterBufferSpaceLeft		dc.w 0								;bytes left in printer's rx bufferSccCommandRegisterSPtr		dc.w 0								;I/O loc of cmd reg for SCC channelSccDataRegisterSPtr			dc.w 0								;I/O loc of data reg for SCC channelSerialFirmwareInitSPtr		dc.w 0								;SCC setup F/W short addressSerialFirmwareInitXReg		dc.w 0								;SCC setup F/W expected X on entrySerialFirmwareInitYReg		dc.w 0								;SCC setup F/W expected Y on entryConnectionIsOpen			dc.w 0								;set if inside CommOpen-Close scopeConnectionAbort				dc.w 0								;set if abort requested or necessaryStatusProcPtr				dc.l 0								;points to pr err status routine/NIL							endproc							end